<Layout>
    <h1>About Us</h1>
    <p>
        Hello! I am Grant Willey, a student at WNCC pursuing my Associates of Science in Computer Science. 
        I am passionate about technology and the way we interact with it, and I am particularly interested in how technology can be used to enhance learning and research in psychology.
        I am committed to using my skills and knowledge to contribute to the field of psychology through technological innovation.
    </p>
    <h3>References</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.53841/bpsper.2026.50.1.53">
            Kaplunov, E., & Ntasioti, A. (2026). The influence of AI tools on critical thinking in higher education. Psychology of Education Review, 50(1), 53–59. https://doi.org/10.53841/bpsper.2026.50.1.53
        </a></li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/07319487261439132">
            Seung, Y., & Basham, J. D. (2026). Cognitive offloading in the age of generative AI: What does it mean for students with learning disabilities? Learning Disability Quarterly, 49(3), 121–133. https://doi.org/10.1177/07319487261439132    
        </a></li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/07319487251412879">
            Shin, M., Deniz, F., Watson, L., Dieterich, C., Ewoldt, K. B., Johnson, F., Kong, J. E., Lee, S. H., & Whitehurst, A. (2026). Addressing the void of AI policies in education for students with specific learning disabilities. Learning Disability Quarterly, 49(3), 134–146.
        </a></li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/bs16040530">
            Zhu, X., Zhang, A., & Zhang, J. (2026). The double-edged sword of AI efficiency: Self-efficacy erosion as a mediator linking instant gratification and perceived AI efficacy to AI dependency. Behavioral Sciences, 16(4), 530.</a></li>
    </ul>
</Layout>

